<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Every entry here names the bug pattern (and, where narrow, the class) and the reason - a silent
  exclude is the same "nothing catches it" problem this plugin exists to close. Nothing here was
  suppressed because it was inconvenient; each entry was read and verified safe, or is a documented,
  deliberate trade-off for this codebase's architecture.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>
    <!--
      SiteScopeGuc concatenates a String into "SET LOCAL app.site_scopes = '...'" because SET LOCAL
      takes no bind parameter (PostgreSQL syntax, not a driver limitation) - documented at length in
      the class's own Javadoc. The value is not escaped, it is *constrained*: encode() drops any scope
      that fails isPlainIdentifier first, which allows only [A-Za-z0-9-_.] - none of which can close a
      quoted string literal, start a new statement, or otherwise change what the statement does.
      SpotBugs's SQL_NONCONSTANT_STRING_PASSED_TO_EXECUTE pattern matches "any non-constant String
      reaching execute()" and cannot see that the allowlist upstream makes injection impossible; a
      bind-parameter fix does not exist for this specific statement shape.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="gh.edu.clet.sfl.common.security.SiteScopeGuc"/>
        <Bug pattern="SQL_NONCONSTANT_STRING_PASSED_TO_EXECUTE"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      AmqpFacilitiesEventTransport.awaitConfirmation (and the fleet/emergency transports built the same
      way) end with:
        (confirm == null || !confirm.isAck()) ? ... : (confirm == null ? "..." : confirm.getReason())
      confirm.getReason() only ever executes when confirm is provably non-null (the ternary's own
      condition guards it) - SpotBugs's null-flow analysis loses that correlation across the outer ||
      and the nested ternary and reports a false null dereference. Read line by line at each of the
      three call sites and confirmed unreachable.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH_FROM_RETURN_VALUE"/>
        <Method name="awaitConfirmation"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      EI_EXPOSE_REP / EI_EXPOSE_REP2, repository-wide.

      This project is a layered Spring application: every controller is constructed with the
      application services it calls, every service with the repositories/ports/outbox/clock it needs,
      every repository adapter with its JdbcTemplate or JPA repository - all stored as final
      constructor-injected fields, which is the intended and only shape dependency injection takes
      here. SpotBugs's EI_EXPOSE_REP2 does not distinguish "a Spring-managed singleton collaborator"
      from "a mutable value object a caller could still reach into and corrupt" - it fires on storing
      *any* externally-supplied reference type outside a small built-in safe list, so it flags a
      constructor storing a FacilitiesRepository exactly the same way it would flag one storing a
      caller-owned ArrayList. There is no meaningful "defensive copy" of a repository or a service
      bean, and excluding this pattern per-class across every constructor in the reactor (on the order
      of a hundred call sites, confirmed by an unfiltered run) would make the filter file larger and
      less readable than the source it is meant to keep legible.

      The same pattern also fires on every Jackson request/response record's List/Map-typed field
      (BookingRequests$AllocateResources.resources, ReadinessRequests$SubmitAssessment.answers, and
      similar). These are deserialized fresh per HTTP request by Jackson and never cached or shared
      across requests, so the realistic exposure window is effectively zero - unlike a value object
      such as SlaPolicy, which this pass fixed properly (Map.copyOf in its compact constructor)
      precisely because it is deliberately built once and reused across many evaluations in one run,
      which is exactly the shape of shared-mutable-state bug this whole hardening pass has been about.

      Net effect of this exclusion: a genuinely new mutable-collection-exposure bug in a class that
      does NOT fit the "DI collaborator" or "ephemeral per-request DTO" shape - most likely another
      cached/reused value object like SlaPolicy - will not be caught by this rule automatically. That
      is the deliberate trade-off being made here, not an oversight: it was judged cheaper to keep
      reviewing that specific, narrower risk by hand (as this pass did for SlaPolicy) than to either
      hand-fix every DI constructor and DTO record in the reactor, or hide a hundred-plus false
      positives behind individual per-class entries in this file.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
      CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW on FleetDomainException: it is `public abstract class ... extends
      RuntimeException` - the documented base type for every fleet business exception - with
      `protected` constructors meant only for its subclasses to call. Its constructor validates
      (Objects.requireNonNull on errorCode) and so can throw, which is exactly what
      CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW flags as a finalizer-attack vector: a subclass constructor that fails
      partway can leave a partially-initialized instance reachable if a malicious subclass overrides
      finalize(). That threat model requires an attacker able to load their own subclass into the same
      JVM, which does not apply here - every subclass is our own compiled domain code, this
      application does not run untrusted plugins/classloaders, and the class cannot be made final
      (it is abstract by design). Compare FleetAccessPolicy, which fixed the same finding by going
      final instead, because it did not need to be subclassed at all.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="gh.edu.clet.sfl.fleetlogistics.fleet.domain.exception.FleetDomainException"/>
        <Bug pattern="CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW"/>
    </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
